35: ### 3.1 Population 1 -- the FAULT ruling is UPHELD, as "an access the emulator cannot service"
36: 
37: Every warrant the owner's ruling rests on is intact here: `memory_rw.c:595-624` converts `return 0` to `M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS` (READ); the same MVME187 constructor arm installs `dev_unreadable` three times for OMITTED hardware (`machine_mvme88k.c:106, :120, :123`), which the seat sharpened into "faults for functionality omitted from emulation"; and the only in-tree guest access to this region is none (vme.c touches offsets <= 0x88; `vmematch` sets `ca_size = 0x100`). The seat's refusal stands and must stay in the record: this is a statement about the EMULATED transaction, never about the real chip (which the local manual cannot describe -- ELSE #2). Nothing to re-rule.
38: 
39: ### 3.2 Population 2 -- NOT reached; the warrant is contradicted; back to the owner with the evidence
40: 
41: **The ruling's stated model of the access is a PROBE.** The ledger note: *"the guest takes the bus error that `badaddr()` probing expects"*; the row title: *"a guest probing an unimplemented VME2 register"*. The seat conditioned its FAULT choice on exactly that model and said what would weaken it (`codex_faultreopen:26, :30`): *"For a driver operating outside a probe, the cost is substantially greater. The exception can abort an operation or ... stop the guest"* and *"A legitimate driver operation could therefore receive an exception solely because this emulator is incomplete."* The seat could not test it -- the guest source was outside its five permitted files -- and nobody else did.
42: 
43: **Tested now, by reading the in-tree guest** (`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/mvme88k/`, the same source `#501` used as a warrant on this board):
44: 
45: * `conf/M187:94` (also GENERIC:69, NFS:95): `vme0 at mainbus0 addr 0xfff40000` -- the driver is configured.
46: * `dev/vme.c:73-103 vmematch()`: **no access at all** -- it sets `ca_paddr`, `ca_size = 0x100`, `ca_bustype = BUS_PCCTWO` and returns 1. No `badaddr`/`badvaddr` call anywhere in `dev/vme.c`, `dev/mainbus.c` or `mvme88k/*.c` (grep). **The in-tree guest never probes the VME2 window.** (The `case 0:` comment "used by OpenBSD/mvme88k when probing" describes some later OpenBSD; whichever it is, offset 0 is served either way.)
47: * `vmeattach():346-352` -> `vme2chip_init():443-543`, unconditional once matched. Its population-2 accesses, all word-width (`vme.h:140-229`: every `struct vme2reg` field is `volatile u_long`), none inside `#if 0`:
48:   - `:452  ctl = vme2->vme2_masterctl;` -- READ 0x28, the FIRST uncased access
49:   - `:467  vme2_master3 = ...` W 0x1c;  `:480 vme2_master4 = ...` W 0x20;  `:482 vme2_master4mod = ...` W 0x24
50:   - `:494  vme2_masterctl = ctl;` W 0x28
51:   - `:576  vme2_irqstat & VME2_IRQ_AB` R 0x68 and `:581 vme2_irqclr = VME2_IRQ_AB` W 0x74 -- `vme2abort`, the abort-switch NMI handler installed at `:539`
52:   (the `master1`/`master2` reads at `:456-463` are inside `#if 0` and compiled out -- I counted them first and corrected it by reading the guards.)
53: * What the guest does with a bus error there: `mvme88k/trap.c:264-369`, kernel-mode `T_DATAFLT`. Recovery exists for exactly three cases -- `pcb_onfault` set by fusu/copyin (`:304-306`, `:368`), a bus error (dpfsr code 0x3) whose `sxip` lies in `guarded_access_start..end` (`:326-336`), or a seg/page fault that `vm_fault` resolves (`:342-363`). `vme2chip_init` is none of them -> `panictrap()` at `:368-369`. (`badaddr()` itself recovers differently and WOULD work: `eh.S:740-827` sets `FLAG_IGNORE_DATA_EXCEPTION` in SR1 and the data-exception prologue's `Data_Precheck` RTEs to `badaddr__return_nonzero` (`:699-717, :820`) with no DPFSR dependency -- which is the half of the premise that is true, and the half nothing in this driver uses.)
54: * Emulator side (READ): `cpu_m88k.c:908-917` loads DMT0/DMD0/DMA0 on `M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS`; no `dpfsr` token exists anywhere in `src/cpus` or `src/include` (grep -ri). So the delivered exception can never take trap.c's guarded-bus-error path either. Same conclusion by two routes: a non-probe kernel access that faults does not come back.
55: 
56: **So the measurable consequence of FAULT on population 2 is:** the in-tree guest's `vme0` attach, which today ends the HOST at `vme.c:452` (MASTERCTL read -> `default:` -> `exit(1)`), would instead end the GUEST at the same instruction. That is strictly better than process death and strictly worse than the alternative the file's own siblings already implement -- and it was never put to the owner. *This is a reading; no mvme187 boot runs in this harness (`dev_pcc2.c:92-93`), so the rung-3 probe the row already owes is where it becomes a measurement.*
57: 
58: **Why population 2 is not "the ruling's strongest instance" (the reading I held for the first ten minutes and had to drop):** the seat's omitted-Ethernet precedent is real, but `unreadable` there replaces a device the guest PROBES before use (`if_ie.c:407-409` fails its match on `badpaddr()`, CHANGELOG `:9325-9326`). Population 2 is reached by a driver that has ALREADY matched (on offset 0, served) and is programming the chip. The precedent transfers to probed absences, not to post-match register traffic. The carrier's companion rule applies verbatim: *"BEFORE TRANSFERRING A PRECEDENT, GREP THE ROUND'S OWN FINDINGS AGAINST EACH WARRANT ELEMENT"* -- the "probing" element was never grepped against the guest.
59: 
60: **The sibling-handler test, which the option-set rule mandates and which settles the DEFAULT:** the five cased-but-unimplemented registers in this very file (GCSRCTL, TCR, IRQEN, IRQL3, IRQL4) DECLINE with an announce and `break` to the common tail. Population 2 is the same class of register without a case label. FAULT-for-population-2 would make MASTERCTL (0x28) fault while GCSRCTL (0x2c, the next word) declines, with the line between them drawn by which labels upstream happened to type. And FAULT-for-ALL-unimplemented is refuted by the same driver one line later: `:496 ctl = vme2->vme2_gcsrctl` is a cased read that would then panic too. `#501` on the same board resolved the identical shape -- eighteen NAMED registers the model does not implement -- as **member 3, DECLINE + latched announce per offset, NOT member 6**, and its fable5 adjudication's test was *"the fix refuses NOTHING the in-tree guest does"* (`:9730`).
61: 
62: **Ruling on population 2:** the owner's FAULT ruling does NOT govern it. It is RE-PUT to the owner with the `vme.c` reading above inlined (it is one paragraph and six line citations). Pending that re-ruling the default is the file's and the board's own idiom: DECLINE + latched announce per offset, reads `odata = 0` set explicitly in the arm (the seat's finding (A): the preload at `:65-66` otherwise serves the containing word), writes leave `d->reg[]` untouched.
63: 
64: **Being harder on the queue author's leaning -- BACKED ACCEPT is NOT licensed, and "available under the addition licence" has it backwards.** The licence demands a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT. There is no measurement (no boot runs). The only candidate warrant is the driver's RMW of MASTERCTL -- and a backed MASTERCTL buys the guest nothing it can use, because every VME window it would program maps onto `unreadable` space (`machine_mvme88k.c:120, :123`). Meanwhile the population contains registers a blanket store MISREPRESENTS: `vme.h:228-229` marks SETSOFTIRQ as *"VME2_IRQ_SWx only"* and IRQCLR as a clear register, IRQSTAT is status -- the same "cased siblings contradict a blanket store" warrant that defeated member 6 at `#501` (`:9314-9316`), and the design seat's own candidate (d) verdict: *"Invents persistent, RAM-like register semantics. Firmware can then rely on fabricated state."* If the owner wants the master-map registers backed, that is a per-register IMPLEMENT decision (member 7, four registers), licensed by the driver reading -- not a population-wide BACKED ACCEPT.
65: 
66: ### 3.3 Population 3 -- outside the ruling; "serve the containing word" is refuted; FILE it
67: 
68: **Not reached:** the ruling's contract is "an access the emulator cannot service." A byte at 0x51 is an access the emulator can service -- the datum is in `d->reg[0x14]` -- and merely does not DECODE. That is the `#337`/`#497`/`#529`/`pcc2widthkills` class (an omitted WIDTH of a present register), which this project has disposed four times and never once by FAULT.
69: 
70: **"Serve the containing word" (the queue's "obvious alternative") is a SKETCH that fails the enclosing function, by reading** (`dev_vme.c:85-97`, `memory.c:55-78, :89-109`):
71: 1. a byte/halfword WRITE rounded down to a storing case executes `d->reg[w] = idata` -- the composed 1- or 2-byte value overwrites the WHOLE 32-bit word; three (or two) lanes are clobbered with zeros and the written lane lands in the LOW end, which on this big-endian bus is the highest-address lane (`memory_readmax64` composes `x = x<<8 | buf[i]`);
72: 2. a byte READ at +1/+2/+3 returns `memory_writemax64(len=1, odata)`, i.e. the LEAST significant byte of the word = lane +3, whatever lane was asked for;
73: 3. a byte read at 0x55-0x57 fires T1COUNT's `+= 100` read side effect (`:95`) once per lane read.
74: The seat's body already said it (`codex_faultreopen:63`): *"Simply rounding the switch address down would be insufficient: current storing cases assign whole words, and T1COUNT has a read side effect."* The queue read the verdict line ("handle register byte lanes separately") and supplied a fix the body refutes -- the carrier's verdict-line hazard, in the queue file.
75: 
76: **"Arguably a worse wrong answer than the host kill it replaces" is rejected.** A localised, survivable guest bus error is not worse than ending the emulator for every guest and every row; the sentence compares a host consequence with a guest one. What IS true: whether a sub-word LCSR access is legal on the real VMEchip2 is NOT ESTABLISHED and cannot be established from disk (ELSE #2) -- the PCCchip2 "can be accessed as bytes (8 bits), two-bytes (16 bits), or four-bytes" sentence at `vmesbcp2.txt:4655-4656` is PCCchip2's, not VMEchip2's, and must not be transferred.
77: 
78: **Disposition:** population 3 is its OWN ROW (name is the main loop's; the shape is `crimectllane`/`pcc2widecompose`). The reopening rule forbids folding it into the FAULT round: different semantic class (width vs. unimplemented register) and a DIFFERENT ORACLE (no exception + unchanged store + one announce, versus an exception). The FAULT round therefore:
79: * DISCLOSES in its contract that sub-word accesses inside cased words reach the fault, as an emulator limitation, not a hardware claim;
80: * carries ONE row that pins that interim behaviour and is NAMED as a channel the width row will red and re-derive (exactly how `#529`'s `sw08/sh08` rows are recorded in `crimectllane`);
81: * records the warrant for deferring rather than repairing: the in-tree guest makes NO sub-word VME2 access (every `vme2reg` field is `u_long`; no `vme2_` reference exists outside `dev/vme.[ch]` -- grep), so FAULT there breaks no in-tree guest path, which population 2 cannot say.
82: The width row's own default, when it runs, is the house idiom -- width declined BEFORE the switch with a latched announce (`#529`), reads lane-extracted from `d->reg[]` where that is `ACCEPT`-shaped (member 4), COMPOSE for writes filed as the faithful upgrade -- but that is that row's panel to settle, after the VMEchip2 chapter is on disk.
83: 
84: ### 3.4 The rule minted, and its bound
85: 
86: > **A disposition ruling binds the population it was argued on.** When an arm is later shown to mix populations, each population not presented is tested against the ruling's STATED WARRANTS, never against its conclusion: a population the warrants reach is covered without re-ruling; a population a warrant CONTRADICTS goes back to the owner with the contradicting evidence inlined; a population the warrants do not touch gets its own row. The partition is read off the code -- the header, the switch, the access width -- so it is finite, and the obligation is one re-ruling or one row PER POPULATION, never per offset.
87: 
88: Warrant in the carrier's own text: the premise-audit rule (*"a stated premise is load-bearing for every seat, and agreement with it is not evidence"* -- here the premise was "probing"); the option-set rule's two greps (the fork's idiom, the SIBLING HANDLERS' disposition -- both run above); the provisional-adjudication companion (grep the round's findings against each warrant element); and the late-dissent discriminator (*can it state a measurable consequence?* -- `vme.c:452`, yes). Bound against `#392`: three populations, one re-ruling, two filed rows, zero rows added to the FAULT round beyond the single channel row an existing precedent already prescribes; population 1 and the FAULT mechanism are not reopened.
89: 
90: ## 4. Measurable consequences, in order
91: 
92: 1. **Before any edit:** re-put population 2 to the owner with section 3.2 inlined (the six accesses, `trap.c:368`, the sibling-parity point, the `#501` precedent). Hold the FAULT edit on that answer -- the ruling was issued on a presentation the queue author himself calls wrong.
93: 2. **The rung-3 probe the row already owes gains a driver-shaped row:** a guest word `ld` at `0xfff40028` (MASTERCTL, the attach's first uncased access) with the observable under each candidate (exception vector vs. served zero), plus the offset-0 and TCTL accesses as the served controls. Pre-fix it kills the host (the symptom); that is the witness this row has never had.
94: 3. **FILE population 3** as its own row carrying: the 40-start deriver, the zero in-tree sub-word accesses, the absent manual chapter, and the `crimectllane` channel obligation. The FAULT round's probe header discloses it and names its one channel row.
95: 4. **FILE population 4** (ELSE #1 below) -- cased-base accesses at `len != 4` served lane-wrong, silent. Reading only; not measured.
96: 5. **Records (L4, in place):** the ledger note's *"the guest takes the bus error that `badaddr()` probing expects"* gains the qualifier that the in-tree guest does not probe this window and travels six uncased registers unconditionally; the carrier's `vmesbcp2.pdf` entry is corrected (ELSE #2); the 7n-4 text's "arguably worse than the host kill" is dropped when the row is filed.
97: 6. **Fetch the full VMESBCP2 (or its Chapter 4)** on the carrier's own vmesbcp2/21066/ddi0406 precedent, with the owner's approval as before, BEFORE the population-3 row is designed -- the access-width question cannot be answered from disk.
98: 
99: ## 5. Something ELSE -- four findings outside the question
100: 
101: **ELSE #1 -- a FOURTH population, unfiled: accesses AT a cased base with `len != 4` are served with word semantics and are lane-wrong, silently.** By reading `dev_vme.c:62-66, :85-101, :131-135, :146-147` with `memory.c:55-109`: a guest `st.b` of 0x12 at 0x88 sets the whole VBR word to `0x00000012` (VBR0/VBR1 wiped); a guest `ld.b` at 0x88 returns 0x00 -- the low byte of `0x67000000` -- where the big-endian bus's byte 0 is 0x67; an 8-aligned `st.d` at 0x50 (len 8 passes the alignment check at 0x50, 0x60, 0x88) stores the doubleword's LOW word into T1CMP and leaves T1COUNT untouched. No kill, no announce, no row. `grep dev_vme ledger.json` finds only `vmedefaultkill`. It is `pcc2widthkills`' own finding (*"(uint8_t) idata is the HIGHEST-address lane"*) in the file next door, unguarded. The in-tree guest never does it (all `u_long`), so LOW/MED -- but it is the sibling of population 3 and the width row should take both.
102: 
103: **ELSE #2 -- the board manual on disk has NO VMEchip2 chapter, and the carrier's entry is wrong twice.** RAN: a per-chapter page-footer census of `_scratchpad/vmesbcp2.txt` (`grep -cE '^\s*N-[0-9]+\s*$'`): chapters 1-3: 0 each; **chapter 4: 1** (and that one, `:10405`, is an index line); 5: 70; 6: 52; 7: 12; 8: 38; 9: 9. The first body footer is `5-1` at `:907`, extracted page 25 of **240** (form feeds 240 = `/Type /Page` objects 240; the carrier says "172 pp"). Only 6 near-empty pages, so chapter 4 is not image-only -- it is physically absent from the PDF. `VMEchip2` occurs only in the TOC (`:399 Chapter 4 VMEchip2`, `:857-861` Tables 4-1/4-2/4-5 at 4-22/4-24/4-110), the index, and two cross-references; `$FFF40088` appears once, in the PCCchip2 chapter. So the carrier's instruction *"A round about PCC2/CD2401/MVME187 opens THIS before reasoning from the source alone"* cannot be followed for THIS device: the LCSR map, the DMA/T2/prescaler registers and the LCSR access-width rule are not on disk. It is the `idt_r30xx.txt`-was-empty class one level finer: a listed manual can be a PARTIAL extract missing the chapter you need, and `wc -c` does not catch it -- the footer census does.
104: 
105: **ELSE #3 -- the premise "a guest probing an unimplemented VME2 register" has ZERO instances in the in-tree guest.** Section 3.2 shows it; recorded separately because it is the kind of sentence that gets reused: `badaddr()` recovery is real (`eh.S:740-827`) and this driver never calls it on this window. Whatever later OpenBSD the `case 0:` comment refers to probes OFFSET 0, which is served.
106: 
107: **ELSE #4 -- "FAULT is available on M88K" is true of DELIVERY and silent about RECOVERY.** The emulator sets DMT0/DMD0/DMA0 on DATA_ACCESS (`cpu_m88k.c:908-917`) and models no DPFSR at all (grep -ri over `src/cpus`, `src/include`: nothing). OpenBSD 2.2's probe path needs no DPFSR (SR1 flag), so probes recover; its non-probe kernel path keys on DPFSR and `pcb_onfault` and cannot. The carrier's "CHECK IT IS AVAILABLE ON THIS CPU" caveat should read "...and check what the guest does with it outside a probe" -- for FAULT the second half is where this round's answer actually lived.
108: 
109: ## 6. What I READ vs what I RAN
110: 
111: READ (no edits anywhere): `GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c` (all), `src/include/thirdparty/mvme88k_vme.h` (all), `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:540-634`, `src/core/memory.c:55-109`, `src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c:898-917`, `src/cpus/cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c:169-172`, `src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c` (grep context), `src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:55-134`, `tools/pipeline/ledger.json:45530-45544, :55896-55900, :35236-35244, :32566-32571`, `tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md:2468-2532`, `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:18805-18874, :18898-18911, :20738-20755`, `CHANGELOG.md:9283-9352`, `regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/codex_design_20260911.txt` and `codex_faultreopen_20260911.txt` (both in full; `.err` tails only), `_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/mvme88k/dev/vme.c:70-103, :318-586`, `dev/vme.h:136-235`, `mvme88k/trap.c:255-375`, `mvme88k/eh.S:395-444, :690-834`, `conf/M187|GENERIC|NFS|files.mvme88k` (grep), `_scratchpad/vmesbcp2.txt:4596-4675, :6650-6674, :7560-7604` plus greps, `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md:1-5`.
112: 
113: RAN (read-only commands, all in my own shell, none touching a build tree): `git --no-optional-locks log` (repo head and `dev_vme.c` history); greps listed above; a python deriver for the population sizes (section 2); a python/grep census of the manual's page footers, form feeds and `/Type /Page` objects (ELSE #2); `ls`/`wc`/`tail` on the artifacts dir. No build, no probe, no gate, no `git` write, no file written to any project tree. The only file written is this one.
114: 
115: What I did NOT do and say so: I did not boot or probe anything, so every guest consequence in 3.2 is a READING of OpenBSD 2.2 and owes the rung-3 measurement the row already carries; I could not read the VMEchip2 chapter because it is not on disk.

